WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.920
Tooling helps teams make unsafe API migration systematic instead of heroic.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:14.820
Compiler warnings can identify deprecated functions, suspicious conversions, missing prototypes, format string mismatches, and other signs that a call site deserves attention.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.560
Warning levels should be chosen deliberately and reviewed regularly.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:30.300
Static analysis can find unchecked return values, risky buffer operations, inconsistent size handling, possible null dereferences, lifetime issues, and suspicious file or process behavior.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:38.040
These findings still need human judgment, but they are valuable because they point reviewers toward patterns that might otherwise blend into familiar code.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.180
Sanitizers and runtime checks can expose memory issues during testing.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.640
They are especially useful when refactoring code that manipulates buffers, strings, and allocation lifetimes.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:54.460
A sanitizer finding should lead to understanding the data contract, not only patching the immediate symptom.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.400
Code search and banned API lists can help teams find and prevent risky patterns.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.980
A banned list should not be a blunt instrument with no escape hatch.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:11.840
It should allow documented exceptions when compatibility or platform constraints require a particular call, and those exceptions should explain the compensating controls.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.860
Continuous integration can enforce the agreed direction.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:21.320
New uses of discouraged APIs can fail review, while existing legacy uses can be tracked in a migration backlog.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:29.060
The key is to make the trend visible: fewer risky calls, better wrappers, stronger tests, and clearer contracts over time.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:36.000
Review checklists should focus on data contracts, error handling, truncation, lifetime, portability, and operational behavior.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:46.220
The checklist should ask what the buffer capacity is, how failures are reported, whether the result may be truncated, who owns memory, and how platform-specific differences are documented.
